    "media:imagetext": "N\nV\nChungking,\nFeb. 28, 1938\nManila, P. I•\nDear Mr. Bixby:\nI have been trying for some time to get up enough courage to\nwrite you a letter containing something more, or less, than a\nmere recital of the events of the day as they take place.\nIt Is now just six months since I landed in China on my return from the\nthe States and all my ideas on the situation out here are as\nvague and shifting now as they were then, probably more so. I have\ntried constantly to {orm/some conclumion, even in the most genefal\nand indefinite terms, as to what the outcome of the present\nconditions in China would be, and how they would effect CNAC and\nwhat would be the best way to meet the situation, but so far I\nhavent been able to reach any conclusion with sufficient clearness\nto form a foundation for any particularly definite course of action.\nAbout all I am really/going on is hope, a term I use with full\nconsideration. Howerer in any circumstances you must haversome\nplan of action however unsatisfactory. Ours is insecurely based\non the guess and hope method. We are simply living and working\nfrom day to day. We are dr course making every effort to be able\nto continue this day to day existence as long in the future as\npossible, but that 1g as far as I have got up to now.\nThere have been many things in this war which have been a\ndisappointment to me and of course there have been some agreeable\nsurprises. The Chinese people as a wh le, according to my ideas,/\nare not in this war and never have been. Maybe it would be fairer\nto say that the Chinese are very much in this war but their\nresistance has been mostly passive resistance. But it is very\ndifficult to win a war with passive resistance, particularly against\nthe Japanese. You may acquire many moral victories and Jou may win\nwhat comes after the war, although I question that also with the\nJapanese. Also it As difficult to win a naz modern war with coolies\nmostly grabbed up off the street. The upper and middle class Chinese\nhave not yet realized that this is really their war and they have\nto furnish the backbone as well as the brains of the fighting 1f\nthey have any hope of winning, It is almost inconceivable for a\nnation to have beenlin such a war as this for seven months, with\nits territory Invaded and its poople to have suffered such horrors\nand barbarous treatment as the Chinese have and yet not find\npractically every ablebodied man between twenty and forty trying\nto get into the army. But I do not know one Chinese who has gone\ninto the fighting forces since this war started or who has a son\nwho has done so. I realize there must be many of the so called\nbetter classes who are in the army but they are comparatively few.",
